Because the controller received a null parameter, the repository ended up saving a null entity. The stack trace shows the controller calls MealServiceImpl.addMeal with a null argument — the usual cause is that the controller method parameter isn't bound to the request body (missing `@RequestBody`) or the request content type doesn't match.

Fix: add `@RequestBody` to the controller method parameter (and optionally `@Valid` if you validate DTOs). Example change for `\backend\src\main\java\...\MealController.java`:

```java
// java
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class MealController {

    private final MealService mealService;

    public MealController(MealService mealService) {
        this.mealService = mealService;
    }

    @PostMapping("/api/meals")
    public ResponseEntity<MealDto> addMeal(@RequestBody MealDto mealDto) {
        MealDto created = mealService.addMeal(mealDto);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(created);
    }
}
```

Also ensure the test sends JSON content type (use `MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON` or `APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE`) and that `TestUtils.asJsonString(...)` produces valid JSON.

The compiler error happens because `public MealDto{` is missing the parentheses — that's the compact-constructor syntax which is only allowed for `record` types. Fix by adding `()` or remove the manual constructor since Lombok's `@NoArgsConstructor` already generates one.

