Because it's not mandatory: you only need a try/catch when you want to translate or handle lower-level runtime exceptions (like `DataAccessException`, `IllegalArgumentException`, `JpaSystemException`) into something meaningful (logging, a user-friendly error, or a specific HTTP status). Better approaches are:

- Validate inputs early (avoid saving a `null` entity).  
- Let exceptions propagate to a global `@ControllerAdvice` or throw `ResponseStatusException` in the controller so the HTTP layer returns proper status codes.  
- Use try/catch only when you need to wrap/log/convert the exception at that layer.

Example — prefer input validation in the service (no try/catch required):

```java
// java
// file: backend/src/main/java/dev/jclawlor/FitnessGoals/service/impl/MealServiceImpl.java
@Override
public MealDto addMeal(MealDto mealDto) {
    java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(mealDto, "mealDto must not be null");
    Meal meal = mapToMeal.mapToMeal(mealDto);
    java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(meal, "mapped Meal must not be null");
    Meal savedMeal = mealRepository.save(meal);
    return mapToMeal.mapToMealDto(savedMeal);
}
```

Example — catch repository exceptions in the controller to return a clear HTTP error:

```java
// java
// file: backend/src/main/java/dev/jclawlor/FitnessGoals/controller/MealController.java
@PostMapping("/api/meals")
public ResponseEntity<MealDto> addMeal(@RequestBody MealDto mealDto) {
    try {
        MealDto created = mealService.addMeal(mealDto);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(created);
    } catch (org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException ex) {
        throw new org.springframework.web.server.ResponseStatusException(
            org.springframework.http.HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, "Database error", ex);
    }
}
```

Use validation to avoid the null/save error; use try/catch only when you need to map or handle specific runtime exceptions.

Making the mapper method static "fixes" the failure because static calls don't rely on Spring autowiring. If your mapper instance wasn't a Spring bean (or the field wasn't injected), calling the instance method would either produce `null` results or a `NullPointerException` and ultimately lead to `save(null)`. Calling a static method bypasses the injection problem and so mapping succeeds.

Do not keep it static as the long-term solution unless you intentionally want a pure utility class. Better options:
- If you use MapStruct, annotate the mapper with `@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")` so Spring creates and injects the bean, and keep instance methods.
- If you wrote the mapper by hand, annotate it with `@Component` (or `@Service`) and keep instance methods.
Static methods reduce testability, flexibility, and prevent Spring features (AOP, proxying) from working.

Example fixes:

MapStruct-based mapper (recommended if using MapStruct). This makes `MealMapper` a Spring bean and lets you inject it with `@Autowired` or constructor injection:

```java
// java
package dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.mapper;

import org.mapstruct.Mapper;
import dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.dto.MealDto;
import dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.entity.Meal;

@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface MealMapper {
    Meal mapToMeal(MealDto dto);
    MealDto mapToMealDto(Meal entity);
}
```

Manual mapper as a Spring component (if not using MapStruct). This ensures the mapper is injected and instance methods are used:

```java
// java
package dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.mapper;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.dto.MealDto;
import dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.entity.Meal;

@Component
public class MealMapper {
    public Meal mapToMeal(MealDto dto) {
        if (dto == null) return null;
        Meal m = new Meal();
        m.setId(dto.getId());
        m.setName(dto.getName());
        // map other fields...
        return m;
    }

    public MealDto mapToMealDto(Meal m) {
        if (m == null) return null;
        MealDto dto = new MealDto();
        dto.setId(m.getId());
        dto.setName(m.getName());
        // map other fields...
        return dto;
    }
}
```

Recommendation: fix the bean configuration and use instance methods. Only use static methods if you deliberately want a stateless utility class and accept the tradeoffs.

Yes — making the mapper static only masked the real issue (the mapper instance wasn't injected). Proper fix: ensure the mapper is a Spring bean and the service uses a single injected mapper (use constructor injection), add null checks, and remove duplicate fields.

Brief explanation: `MealMapper` is annotated as a Spring component so Spring can inject it. `MealServiceImpl` uses constructor injection, removes duplicate mapper fields, validates input, maps, saves, and maps back to DTO.

